WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.152
Before a recruitment event, what gives two people a real reason to meet?

2
00:00:04.402 --> 00:00:08.996
Meet Robin: an operations specialist who wants to develop data skills

3
00:00:08.996 --> 00:00:15.677
Harbor Services needs someone who can handle an incident, keep customers informed, and validate the data

4
00:00:15.677 --> 00:00:16.930
Both are fictional.

5
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:23.654
CommonGround gives each side a digital twin: a representative built from information its owner reviews

6
00:00:23.654 --> 00:00:27.969
Here are Robin and Harbor in the actual local prototype.

7
00:00:28.219 --> 00:00:33.568
Robin's work example describes coordinating an incident and separating facts from assumptions

8
00:00:33.568 --> 00:00:38.026
Harbor needs clear updates when the recovery time is unknown

9
00:00:38.026 --> 00:00:39.809
That is shared ground

10
00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:42.929
But data validation has no supplied example.

11
00:00:43.179 --> 00:00:54.676
The useful output is a question: Could you walk through a concrete example of data validation? Both sides can explore the evidence and whether the role supports Robin's learning goals

12
00:00:54.676 --> 00:00:56.592
Missing evidence stays a question.

13
00:00:56.842 --> 00:01:03.672
In this successful local Gemini run, the twins also rehearse how to discuss an uncertain recovery time

14
00:01:03.672 --> 00:01:08.895
This is hypothetical dialogue, not observed behaviour or a prediction of job success.

15
00:01:09.145 --> 00:01:12.304
The same pair now has a shared agenda

16
00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:15.463
Robin and Harbor each choose whether to meet

17
00:01:15.463 --> 00:01:17.832
These buttons simulate the two parties

18
00:01:17.832 --> 00:01:21.385
A changed twin requires a fresh review and introduction.

19
00:01:21.635 --> 00:01:25.303
Judges can try the browser preview without a key

20
00:01:25.303 --> 00:01:28.971
It uses fictional session data and no live model

21
00:01:28.971 --> 00:01:32.638
The downloadable app adds live dialogue and saved evidence.

22
00:01:32.888 --> 00:01:38.955
Our ask to Espoo: one workshop with an event team, using fictional examples

23
00:01:38.955 --> 00:01:46.888
Compare preparation effort and usefulness before planning a voluntary pilot with the necessary identity and privacy controls

24
00:01:46.888 --> 00:01:47.821
Two twins

25
00:01:47.821 --> 00:01:49.688
A clearer first conversation.

